A||N||3||Taijiguy||Kinda cool idea...monitor the engine compartment temp...||||10||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||05-13-2001||11:06 PM||||Somebody posted a question about a temp gauge to monitor engine compartment temperatures. This has been a particular concern of mine, since with all the other mods I've done, I have no vents back there any more. (solid deck panels and no vents on the deck lid) <BR>So this post (sorry, don't recall who you are that originated it) it got me thinking, and then the light went on. A few years ago my dad, who does most of his Christmas shopping at Quality Farm and Fleet, the same year he got me a Chia-Pet, (and was serious about it) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> also gave me an indoor/outdoor thermometer, which eventually found its way to the bottom of the drawer in my kitchen with the Scotch tape and the pens that don't work, but have cool enough covers I don't wanna throw them away. Well, this thing has a temp range of like, -40 to 185 degrees. And as it turns out, makes a <B>dandy</B> engine compartment temperature monitor. It has a long wire with a remote sensor, and  also has a memory feature, to recall the highest/lowest temperatures.<BR>Radio Shack sells these things, and they're cheap. So if you ever wondered how hot it gets back there, I can tell you today, mine reached 157 degrees   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||JetroGT||05-14-2001||02:59 AM||||157 dosen't sound that hot. I thought for sure it would be alot hotter. I wonder how other cars compare to the fieros engine compartment temps?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||05-14-2001||09:22 AM||||It would be good if you specify where did you put the sensor. I would be interested in the area close to the TBS and intake pipe. That's what heats up the intake charge and robs you HP. With my AutoXray I like to monitor the reading of the air temperature sensor (the one in the filter canister). It would be cool if a difference in avg temp. could be found between the stock unit and a unit like C.West (cold intake tube).<BR>Or maybe monitoring the change of adding more insulation to the stock one. My highest current reading has been near 120°F in an 85°F day during city driving. Dropping that 10°-15° would make a good difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-14-2001||09:32 AM||||I put the sensor on the inside of the deck lid. I was primarily interested in the surface temp of the deck lid, since I figured it would be a fairly accurate representation of the average temp in the engine compartment. I'm not too worried about air intake temperature, since I have a functional hood scoop for cold air intake. You could easily mount the sensor inside the flexible connector  to the TB. The sensor on the one I have is less than 1/2" in diameter, and maybe the thickness of a nickle.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
